experiments/k1303/k1303_codex_review.md:31:- **Finding**: Features use `rv_lag1 = d["rv"].shift(1)` (X_{t-1}) while target is `Y = log(RV.shift(-1))` = log(RV_{t+1}). This means the model predicts two steps ahead from t-1 features — one extra lag beyond standard HAR-CJ (which uses X_t → Y_{t+1}). The extra lag is particularly damaging to jump signals which decay faster than continuous components.
experiments/k1303/k1303_codex_review.md:34:- **Fix**: If goal is standard next-day HAR-CJ, change to X_t → Y_{t+1} (no .shift(1) on features, target still .shift(-1) on rv). If two-step-ahead is intentional, rename as "two-step-ahead HAR-CJ" and do not compare directly against standard HAR-CJ literature results.
experiments/K1322/codex_review.md:12:1. **Lookahead bias** — `rv_d = rv.shift(1)`; `rv_w` / `rv_m` are rolling on `rv_lag1`; target is `log(RV_t)` of same-day. No future leakage.
experiments/k1303/k1303_v2_review.md:37:- `rv_lag1 = d["rv"].shift(1)` → all HAR terms are lag-1 or longer
experiments/k1303/README.md:38:- **Fix**: Target = log(RV_t), features = .shift(1) → standard 1-step HAR per ABD (2007).
experiments/k1303/README.md:63:- All features use `.shift(1)`: feature at row t = value from day t-1.
